mohammed bashir fled his home in kobani  to escape islamic state  since then he's been smoking up to 80  cigarettes a day to cope with the stress  he often comes to this hill on turkey's  border with syria  to look over kobani  mohammed says he'd be smoking and  drinking if alcohol were available  he just wants to forget what's happened  and feel nothing  he says he's angry islamic state is  destroying his country  in front of his eyes  they are murdering stealing taking  everything  here  mohammed points out the restaurant where  he used to work as a chef  the building has been destroyed  we hear gunfire but there is no way to  tell which side it's coming from  muhammad asks the others but they don't  know either  muhammad says he wants to join the fight  but can't because he has young children  his oldest son is still fighting in  kabbani but muhammad had to leave  he brought his family to the turkish  border town of suraj  six weeks ago they were given shelter in  one of the five refugee camps in suruc  more than 5 000 people live in these  hastily built tents  without running water or electricity  lives in this tent with his wife salwa  and eight of his children  salwa says she'd like to offer us tea  but at the moment there's no water  the youngest of muhammad and salwa's  children is just three years old  the parents have had to explain the  harsh realities of their situation to  her  as well  she's told the children that there's a  war going on in kobani  but she says they still ask her why they  can't return  she tells them no there's fighting back  home and islamic state is there  talking about home makes her think about  her 19 year old son ayub  he's still fighting in kobani salwa  doesn't even have a photograph of him  is all she can say  a truck arrives with blankets the nights  are already cold in the tents without  any heating  one man complains that he hasn't got  enough for his family muhammad and salwa  stay out of the dispute  muhammad says it's hard it feels like  begging  before the conflict he had a house and  everything that goes with it  standing here she's embarrassed  finally they receive their blankets but  they only get three  too little for a family of ten  we want to know how aid supplies are  organized so we head to the city centre  suraj is governed by turkish kurds  it's a city of 100 000 currently hosting  some  sixty thousand refugees  people have been flooding into the city  for six weeks  more arrive every day elmes has taken  responsibility for the care and  accommodation the refugees receive  she set up a warehouse for humanitarian  aid in an old factory  most of the supplies were donated by the  kurdish community  the provisions they've collected so far  won't be enough to last the winter  they need better tents heating supplies  food and medicines  put simply they need everything  organizing the process is a logistical  nightmare  the city doesn't have the resources to  cope with the crisis  says only the kurdish community has  supported them so far  before they were financing 20 000 meals  a day  now it's just 10 000. they're having to  split the portions to provide for  everyone  and they only have four vehicles to  distribute the goods  they need help from the international  community elmes is constantly looking  for support  as we're driving she receives a phone  call telling her an austrian delegation  is waiting in her office  the delegation wants to learn about the  situation in kobani  they're joined by a group of journalists  sees a chance to gain international  support  she inquires about aid supplies for the  refugees  and at the same time brings up the issue  of kurdish  independence and why kobani cannot be  allowed to fall  is very important to the kurds if it  falls their 40-year struggle for  independence will have been for nothing  all the victims the arrests all the  suffering she says the fight may go on  for a long time  but the kurds won't abandon kobani  elmes gets constant updates on the  fighting it's a day of mixed feelings  for her  more kurds have been killed in battles  on this day  five men died in the fighting none were  older than 30.  this man says his blood is boiling with  rage they've come here to honor the  young people who have died  this woman asks what islamic state wants  from them and why they want to repress  them  burn their homes down and take their  money she says they claim to be fighting  for islam but they're not muslims  they're infidels  this graveyard was set aside for victims  of the fighting  the sadness here is mixed with anger at  the turkish government  people here feel ankara has done little  to save the city  elmes doesn't want to accept that turkey  will only allow injured  people to cross its border people here  say even those helping is troops will be  allowed to enter turkey  elmer says whatever she does won't be  enough she gives the refugees food and  cares for them  but she still has a guilty conscience  she says if she were asked  she'd like to go to kirbani and fight  herself  we head back to the warehouse to meet  the bashir family again  fourteen-year-old doesn't want to join  the game  she misses her home  she says if she were in kobani she'd be  at her uncle's house  playing with her cousins and friends  their talk listen to music and study  together  we ask her what she misses most  she says listening to music and watching  tv  there's some good news for the children  though soon a makeshift school is due to  open here in the warehouse  they're excited at the thought of going  back to school  at 7 pm the food rations are handed out  muhammad collects them for the family he  receives rice  soup and mandarins enough for everyone  like his daughter he thinks about what  he'd be doing in kirbani right now  cooking in his restaurant  for the time being he and his wife don't  want to think about starting a new life  in turkey  salva says willing she'd return to  kabani soon and finally see her son  again  muhammad says when he returns home he'll  smile again for the first time  his son will get married and they'll  celebrate with him  kurdish forces have been bolstered by  the arrival of peshmerga fighters from  iraq  the refugees take that as a positive  sign  tomorrow mohammed will return to his  spot on the border  and look over the town he hopes will  soon be liberated  foreign
 

